<div class="intro-content axis-examples">
  <section>
    <h2>Behaviour in Amstelvar Avar2</h2>
    <p>
      Amstelvar Avar2 implements XSVA through separate axes for uppercase,
      lowercase, and figures. The transformation changes the horizontal scale
      of vertical serifs while keeping their position in the glyph’s skeletal
      construction optically stable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Straight construction — T</h2>
    <p>
      T is a useful control: the vertical serif becomes narrower or wider
      while its attachment to the vertical stem and its central alignment
      remain stable.
    </p>

{% set specimenAxis = "XSVU" %}
{% set specimenGlyph = "T" %}
{% include "project-axis-specimen-pair.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Multi-arm construction — E</h2>
    <p>
      E tests the relationship between vertical serif width, the main stem,
      and several horizontal arms. The serif must remain balanced within this
      more complex construction.
    </p>

{% set specimenAxis = "XSVU" %}
{% set specimenGlyph = "E" %}
{% include "project-axis-specimen-pair.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Lowercase construction — r</h2>
    <p>
      In r, the vertical serif is controlled independently from uppercase
      forms. Its horizontal extent changes while the serif remains connected
      to the stem and shoulder.
    </p>

{% set specimenAxis = "XSVL" %}
{% set specimenGlyph = "r" %}
{% include "project-axis-specimen-pair.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Figure construction — seven</h2>
    <p>
      Figures have their own vertical-serif width control. Seven tests the
      change in projection while preserving the figure’s intended construction
      and spacing.
    </p>

